WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.360
 So now that we're solving our problem using a series of cooperating applications communicating across a network,

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:11.600
 we're going to talk a little bit more detail about the notion of what we call web services.

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:17.840
 And in this we're going to take a different perspective. Instead of building our application and breaking it into pieces,

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:23.900
 we're going to have an application that's going to really consume an API from somebody else. So there is some other

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:27.080
 provider of this API that's not us.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:33.000
 And so that if you're going to talk to somebody's data like Google or Amazon or

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:38.040
 Twitter, they're going to say you have to use our API. So what's that?

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:43.800
 So an API is a contract that says look if you do this and this and this and this we're going to give you data

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:49.360
 this way. And they set the rules, they tell you what the URLs are, they'll tell you if it's XML or JSON.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:55.400
 And this is called the application program interface. And it's something you read and you understand.

00:00:55.400 --> 00:01:01.240
 And so you go look at the documentation. This is the documentation for the Google Maps API.

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:04.480
 So it turns out that Google knows a lot about maps.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:11.140
 It knows a lot of data, it knows how to search maps, and it actually provides some of those features to you that your

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:15.760
 application can take advantage of. I took advantage of this at one point by

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:20.300
 asking all the students in one section of one of my online courses where they were from.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:25.600
 And I just let them type in where it was. And then I said well, I don't know how to code any of that.

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:31.560
 So I used this API doing what's called geocoding to look all those places up and get precise

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:39.240
 latitudes and longitudes for the ones Google could figure out. And that saved me a lot of work. Now these are expensive resources, but I

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:46.280
 could be patient and make use of these resources, which as long as you use them not too much, they can be free.

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:50.040
 We'll talk a little bit more about rate limiting and what's free and what's not in a bit.

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:55.360
 But you start by reading documentation. It says do this, hit this URL, hit that URL.

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:58.200
 So if you read that documentation,

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:04.280
 you will find that there is a URL that you can hit. And they tell you where to go.

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:11.280
 And then you go to this URL, you add a question mark, and then you say address equals, and then antivirus plus,

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:16.440
 and there's all these rules. These are called URL encoding rules when you have key values on URLs.

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:23.200
 The plus means space and percent 2c means comma. So these are called URL encoded.

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:30.760
 Don't worry too much about that because we're going to have a magic library like we always do in Python that takes care of this.

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:36.160
 And so if you were to hit this URL, you type it in the exact right way in your browser,

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:40.160
 you will get back a JSON document. It's an object that has key value pairs,

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:47.080
 the first value is the status, then it has these results and it's a list, and you dive down and eventually you can kind of find the latitude and

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:54.000
 longitude of the thing that you are looking for. And so the idea is can we write a program that can read this?

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:58.880
 And so here's our little program that reads this. And a lot of this is sort of

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:02.320
 comfortable. You've already seen some of this.

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:05.040
 You import urllib.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:10.600
 We have to parse them JSON, we grab the URL, and then we're going to write a little while loop

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:14.840
 that's going to ask for a location and we can type that location in and

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:17.320
 we've got to concatenate

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:24.360
 with this URL, the location equals, and there is a bit of code, a library that called parse URL and code,

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:26.400
 that takes the key and the value,

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:33.080
 so the address equals and then whatever this text is that we read in from the user, that goes in here and it does that

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:39.760
 URL encoding with the pluses and the percent 2c and all that stuff is taken care of and that is our URL that we're going to pass

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:46.560
 to URL open. So we print out that we're going to retrieve it, prints this out, and if you look at this, it's too long,

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:50.080
 it has all that fancy stuff on it, and then we read it.

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:57.020
 I mean we open it with URL open and then we read it and decode it. So these two things hit this URL,

00:03:57.020 --> 00:03:59.960
 decode it, and then we retrieved

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:06.920
 1669 characters because it's just a, in this case, because we've decoded it, data is a string now,

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:09.680
 that's read as bytes and data is a string.

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:12.240
 So we read

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:18.960
 that many characters, 1669 characters, and then we're going to take this data and we're going to parse it with JSON and

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:25.040
 we might get bad data here, it might blow up, but it might work, and so in this case it works.

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:29.800
 We have an error that basically says if we got a bad thing, we're going to blow up,

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:34.400
 but in this case it doesn't blow up, and so now we're going to sort of dig through and

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:40.200
 if you go back, let's, let me just go back, so the

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:47.800
 results sub zero geometry, let's show you how that works. So results is the first key,

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:54.640
 so this is a dictionary with a key of results, but then it has a list, this list starts here and goes there and

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:58.520
 there's, I'm only going to show part of it, but there's many things here.

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:02.160
 So the zero item is this, this is the sub zero, and

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:07.800
 then geometry within that sub zero item, so if we look at that, it is

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:16.600
 the outer dictionary, the first item in the list, sub geometry, so that grabs one part,

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:19.640
 that grabs

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:22.720
 this part right here, and

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:31.040
 then we're going to go into location and lat, and those are just keys within keys, a dictionary within a dictionary, and so you see it says

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:36.960
 sublocation sub lat, and so that is literally going to pull out of that complex structure,

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:43.640
 that will pull the latitude out, and then in the next line pull the longitude out, so we can pull

00:05:43.640 --> 00:05:51.160
 the latitude and longitude out, and then we print it out. We can go into results sub zero formatted address, and that goes into

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:53.920
 results zero

00:05:53.920 --> 00:06:00.200
 formatted address, and that pulls this little bit out. Now it takes a little while to write this stuff, and you have to put a lot of

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:04.680
 debug, and you don't necessarily figure out this complex bit here at the end,

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:09.200
 but you know, you print it, you don't get what you want, you say, oh wait a sec, that was an array,

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:12.660
 so I got to add a little sub zero there to get the first one out of the array,

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:16.360
 but eventually you figure it out, and it's not all that difficult.

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:20.880
 It's the first time, first few times you do it, like what am I doing, but after a while you realize,

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:26.360
 oh, I'm just sort of tearing this apart and digging deeper and deeper into this data structure,

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:31.800
 which I just retrieved over the internet from Google, and I learned something good from that.

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:34.900
 Now we're going to talk about API rate limiting and security.

00:06:34.900 --> 00:06:42.560
 The key thing is is that the Google API and the Google data is super valuable, and you could build a website that did nothing,

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:47.800
 but sort of like asked the person for something, and then showed them that place and make them be a map searcher,

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:54.120
 and you added so little value and Google did all the hard work, and so they protect these somewhat.

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:59.880
 Sometimes they'll say you can only do 50 of these a day or 500 a day or whatever, that's called rate limiting,

00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:05.920
 and sometimes they say you've got to log in, you've got to create an account and get a key with us and then present your key,

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:12.120
 so that means that your account only gets so many, and they keep track of who's using their service and how much they're using it.

00:07:12.120 --> 00:07:16.560
 Google gives you even sort of a dashboard that tells you some of this stuff, it's kind of nice.

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:27.840
 The other thing is that sometimes an API is free and then it becomes popular and they decide they're going to put a key on it or a rate limit on it,

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:33.520
 so you got to kind of play this game with them and the rules kind of change as things progress.

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:44.360
 So that geocoding API that we're talking about has at one point in time 2,500 requests a day, you can get more requests if you get a key.

00:07:44.360 --> 00:07:49.400
 So this whole web services section, we've done quite a bit of stuff, right?

00:07:49.400 --> 00:08:00.080
 We've looked at how instead of reading HTML or flat text, we are creating structured data according to contracts, whether it be XML or JSON.

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:09.760
 We can retrieve and parse that information in a deterministic way, we talked about schemas that define the contracts so that you know if the data you're getting is wrong,

00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:20.720
 you could know who to blame cuz the schema gets violated and we've played with APIs where you're talking to someone else who's defining what the rules are and how to read their documentation.

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:27.120
 And even if they have an API key or need to sign URLs, showed a little bit about how to do that.

00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:46.400
 [MUSIC]

